#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124223 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "922b0f79-2c87-56f9-bf8a-18711a2a09d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4838" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. What is $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$, we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{C} &= P_{B → C}[x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124224 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "a3911ca2-a357-5f37-8abc-e01e5518f24b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4840" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$. To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{C → B} &= P_{B → C}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/((-4)(-4) - 6 \\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{B} &= P_{C → B}[x]_{C} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124225 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "a2d3629f-5b7c-5b65-998f-8754ff11e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4841" :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}}\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$. As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$. To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S → B} &= P_{B → S}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 \\cdot 10 - 3 \\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{B} &= P_{S → B}[x]_{S} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124302 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "f04f55d3-40ec-52a8-a762-d8ac7090b8b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7276" :question/problem "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$. We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n$\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; c_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}c_{2} & b_{1}\\end{vmatrix}b_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-6)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{C} &= P_{B → C}[x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b20ed1-2ce6-5fb4-bac4-40be33a3c82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294464} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4b6445-8671-5d29-88ab-f549b8ddf893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294465} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7c956d-97f4-5416-9982-9145cb460462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294466} {:answer/id #uuid "52a27d75-4b7b-5e39-825c-1cf4c4c2d76c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294467} {:answer/id #uuid "9a438c04-4c8b-55b9-8a84-1823140209ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a438c04-4c8b-55b9-8a84-1823140209ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294468} :answer-field/id #uuid "c741a8e2-0bfc-5036-a313-875ab9cac0ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "898a9b69-1ce6-510f-b039-ca6d19ae6353" :question/math-academy-id "q-113606" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91464b5f-ff02-517e-b602-2b282f4031eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 22\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122483} {:answer/id #uuid "962dc444-fa59-50b8-a22a-00e27ecba948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122484} {:answer/id #uuid "964494b9-301b-50d4-b337-1923f7356fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122485} {:answer/id #uuid "0887e955-50ac-5551-9861-2f9d77c14401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122486} {:answer/id #uuid "2996e578-f4b9-55be-82a3-8f336b0b508c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2996e578-f4b9-55be-82a3-8f336b0b508c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122487} :answer-field/id #uuid "862933da-272f-5db5-90aa-7c6cc52fd38c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122482}] :question/id #uuid "f503090a-50ea-5c09-941f-e82b557a90db" :question/math-academy-id "q-36503" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}$?\n\n$P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$"}]}
